From 3b5962982939a67885e337ed0693ee2203eaee10 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 20:49:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] The Portal chamber: three portals, seven destinations

The most expensive room in the house - 100,000 coins and 50 Construction - contained four hotspots
that did nothing at all. Clicking any of them fell through to the engine's "Nothing interesting
happens", which is what it says whenever a target op has no trigger.

That is the answer to the wider report, by the way: 93 hotspot Build ops across every house had no
family in furnspec.json, so genfurn.py never emitted a trigger for them. Built furniture is fine -
295 locs, 449 ops, none dead. The hotspots are the dead clicks. This closes four of them; 67 of the
remaining 89 sit in rooms a one-storey house can actually make.

WHAT YOU CAN BUILD. Three focus tiers on the centrepiece (Teleport focus, Greater focus, Scrying
pool) and three frame tiers in each of the three portal spaces (teak, mahogany, marble). Levels,
materials and experience are OSRS's own.

HOW DIRECTING WORKS. What you build is a FRAME; where it leads is decided afterwards at the focus
and costs runes rather than planks - one hundred times the cost of casting that teleport, which is
what OSRS charges. So a directed portal is the same built piece wearing a different loc, which is
why the frame's placement goes through ~poh_portal_place instead of a loc_add literal in the show
switch. genfurn.py learned one new spec key, "show", to route it there.

WHERE THE DESTINATION IS KEPT, and the one compromise in the round. Not in the furniture record:
that word is 30 bits of 32 already and three destinations need nine. %poh_portals is its own perm
varp, and a portal is identified by WHICH TILE it stands on - the template fixes the three spaces
at (0,3), (3,7) and (7,3), so the coord names the portal and nothing has to be stored beside it.
The cost is that two Portal chambers in one house share their three destinations. OSRS keeps them
apart; the record has no room to.

EVERY LEVEL AND LANDING COORD IS ITS OWN TELEPORT SPELL'S, copied out of magic_spells.dbrow rather
than invented - a portal is that spell made permanent, so it asks the same and lands in the same
place. Group 58 checks that against the spell table, which is the check that stops the two drifting.
It also checks that each of the 24 loc cases is its own tier's loc for its own destination, that
the runes taken are the runes checked for, that the three offsets are the template's own, and that
all four destination tables carry a default - 0 means undirected, and every one of them is really
read with it.

Nine mutations prove those go red, including the one that would let a marble portal to Camelot come
up as a teak one to Falador.

rs2check 0 ERROR / 0 CHECK over 1,668 scripts and 459 configs; poh_battery ALL PASS (58 groups);
poh_mutate every mutation caught.

